    "summary": "High‑energy early‑Dead set with stellar Cream Puff War and One Kind Favor",
    "blurb": "The Matrix show is a high‑octane three‑set romp packed with early blues and folk numbers. Standout moments include a ripping Cream Puff War solo, the raucous Alice D. Millionaire and an inspired One Kind Favor. Overall playing is tight and spirited, making it a solid entry for anyone digging the 1966 era.",
    "review": "December 1, 1966 at The Matrix captures the Grateful Dead in a raw yet electrifying phase of their evolution. From the opening New Minglewood Blues through a kaleidoscope of blues‑heavy cuts and folk‑rock gems, the band rides an unrelenting wave of energy. Jerry’s guitar shines on Cream Puff War with a blistering solo that still turns heads, while his playful lick in One Kind Favor adds a dash of fun to the set. Pigpen’s organ and harmonica drive the blues numbers, especially on Alice D. Millionaire and Death Don’t Have No Mercy, where his gritty textures lock into Bill’s tight drumming. Phil lays down a solid low‑end foundation that anchors the extended jams, and the rhythm section stays locked in from start to finish. Though the tape quality varies—some recordings suffer from hiss or pitch quirks—the overall performance is vibrant, making this night a must‑listen for fans of the early Dead.",
